{{Elections in Illinois}}

The '''1990 United States Senate election in Illinois''' was held on November 6, 1990. Incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] [[United States Senate|U.S. Senator]] [[Paul Simon (politician)|Paul Simon]] sought re-election to a second term in office. Simon was opposed by [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee [[Lynn Morley Martin]], a [[Member of Congress|U.S. Congresswoman]] from [[Illinois's 16th congressional district]], whom he easily defeated to win a second and final term in the Senate. Martin carried only two counties, [[Edwards County, Illinois|Edwards]] and [[McHenry County, Illinois|McHenry]].

Primaries were held March 20, 1990.<ref name="primary"/>

==Background==
The primaries and general elections coincided with those for [[1990 United States House of Representatives elections in Illinois|House]], as well as those for [[1990 Illinois elections#State elections|state offices]].

For the primaries, turnout was 23.02%, with 1,384,324 votes cast.<ref name="primary">{{cite web|last=Michaelson|first=Ronald D. (Executive Director)|title=Official Vote Cast at the Primary Election Held on March 20, 1990|page=16|date=June 13, 1990|url=https://archive.org/details/stateofillinoiso1990illi/mode/2up |publisher=Illinois State Board of Elections |access-date=June 17, 2024|via=[[Internet Archive]]}}</ref> For the general election, turnout was 53.90%, with 3,251,005 votes cast.<ref name=general>{{cite web|last=Michaelson|first=Ronald D. (Executive Director)|title=Official Vote Cast at the General Election November 6, 1990|date=January 17, 1991|publisher=Illinois State Board of Elections|url=https://archive.org/details/officialvote90illi/mode/2up|access-date=June 17, 2024|via=[[Internet Archive]]}}</ref>

