==General election ==
===Candidates ===
* [[Paul Simon (politician)|Paul Simon]] (D), incumbent [[United States Senator]]
* [[Lynn Morley Martin]] (R), [[United States Congresswoman]] from [[Illinois's 16th congressional district]]

===Election===
At the start of the election, Martin was considered a formidable challenger, but her campaign floundered – in ads, Martin poked fun at Simon's signature [[bow tie]], but the ad campaign, an attempt at humor, was seen by some as petty and mean-spirited. Martin's campaign suffered from poor fundraising as well, being outspent by Simon by a margin of two-to-one. Simon's popularity proved too much to overcome, and he won with 65 percent of the vote, carrying all but two counties in the state; [[Edwards County, Illinois|Edwards County]] in the southeast and [[McHenry County, Illinois|McHenry County]] outside Chicago, in the heart of the district Martin represented for most of the 1980s. In a midterm favorable to Democrats, Martin was further hurt by negative campaign tactics deployed by advisor [[Roger Ailes]], as well as a number of gaffes. Including, referencing downstate voters as "rednecks".<ref name="High Hopes">{{cite news |last1=Oreskes |first1=Michael |title=High Hopes of Defeating Simon Are Being Deflated |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/10/19/us/high-hopes-of-defeating-simon-are-being-deflated.html |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=October 19, 1990 |access-date=June 17, 2024}}</ref> Martin raised the most campaign funds out of any Republican Senate challenger that cycle.<ref name="High Hopes"/>

